[                survived] XIV.1 Linear classes g = theta.F, |theta - theta0| <= w: without evaluations sup (g(x) - g(y)) = centre + sum_a w_a |F_a(x) - F_a(y)| (attained); after evaluations on A the largest consistent value at y is min_lam [lam.v + theta0.r + w.|r|] and the smallest max_lam [lam.v + theta0.r - w.|r|], r = F(y) - sum_i lam_i F(x_i) (linear-programming duality at both ends, also for data at a vertex of the box; every lam gives a valid bound); the width is at most twice the weighted l1 distance of F(y) to the span, with equality at the central data and strict inequality in general; the value is determined exactly when F(y) lies in the span of the evaluated feature vectors; and with a first-order remainder the upper end gains the transport term sum_i (lam_i^+ d(y,x_i) + lam_i^- d(x_i,y)) and the lower end the transposed one, for coefficients summing to one  (3.0s) pairs=80, brackets=169, exact=70, nontrivial=62, remainder=20, narrower=9
[                survived] XIV.2 Pair-additive residuals g(x) = sum_k phi(s_k) cnt_k(x) with phi = 0 at the cutoff and |phi(s_k) - phi(s_{k+1})| <= L (s_{k+1} - s_k): g(x) = sum_k (phi_k - phi_{k+1}) N_k(x) with N the cumulative pair counts; sup (g(x) - g(y)) = L sum_k Delta_k |N_k(x) - N_k(y)| (a transport distance between pair-distance distributions, attained); after evaluations the largest consistent value at y is min_lam [lam.v + L sum_k Delta_k |N_k(y) - sum_i lam_i N_k(x_i)|]; and for a radial function that vanishes at the cutoff and is nondecreasing, a configuration whose cumulative profile dominates another one has the smaller energy  (0.7s) identities=10, envelopes=40, brackets=62, open_brackets=34, dominances=102
[                survived] XIV.3 Linear-residual kernel (LinearKernel.lean): on a coefficient box [lo, hi] with nonnegative features, min_x (E0 + lo.F)(x) + lam.v - sum_a max(lo_a c_a, hi_a c_a), c = F(A)^T lam, is a lower bound on min_x (E0 + theta.F)(x) for every theta of the box that is consistent with the anchors, for every lam; on boxes that contradict the anchors the data term is unbounded; and on small boxes around a consistent theta the bound approaches the true minimum  (1.8s) leaves=48, contradicted=5, tight=12, improved=5, smallest_gain=28.84
[                survived] XIV.4 Pair-distance profiles on real chains (self-avoiding walks on the square lattice, squared distances, cutoff S_c): for an L-Lipschitz radial function with phi(S_c) = 0 the pair-additive energy is -int phi' N^x; the envelope L int |N^x - N^y| equals L times the l1 distance of the sorted squared distances truncated at the cutoff and the supremum over the extreme radial functions; mirror images and reversed chains have equal profiles; and (entropy lemma of Proposition 11.49) rounding a nondecreasing function of total mass M on [0, S] to G levels and G grid points changes it by at most 2SM/G in L1 and leaves at most C(2G, G) functions  (2.0s) walks=1281, profiles=149, pairs=180, rounded=60, worst_rounding=0.7938
[                survived] XV.1 The identity behind the adaptive exponent (theory report 11.18): KL(rho||p_u) = KL(rho||1/2) + u rho - Lambda1, hence theta_hint(e) = theta_na(e + theta_hint(e)) on [0, eps*) and theta_na(h) = h with 0 < h < a; e + theta_hint(e) is nondecreasing, theta_hint lies above its tangent at 0, whose slope is above -1, theta_na is nonincreasing, at most a and vanishes continuously at eps*; and one step of the level recursion from theta_hint reproduces theta_hint at phi = g/(g + theta_hint(g)), beta -> 0, and is never below it for other parameters (theta_hint is a fixed point)  (0.2s) identities=246, steps=48, closest=0.0002806, largest_slope=0.4999
[                survived] XV.2 Level recursion T_1 = theta_na, T_{k+1} = min(T_k, R[T_k]), R[T](g) = inf_{phi, beta} sup_{gamma >= beta} [min(gamma, phi theta_na((g - beta)/phi)) + (1 - phi) T(gamma/(1 - phi))]: the levels are nonincreasing in k and in the accuracy and never below theta_hint; the second level at vanishing accuracy is min_phi max((1 - phi) a, phi a + (1 - phi) theta_na(phi a/(1 - phi))), about a/2, and improves on theta_na by at least 10% up to half the threshold; step (iii) of the convergence proof, D_{k+1}(g) <= (1 - phi*(g)) max_{x <= g} D_k(x), holds; and after six levels the excess over theta_hint is below 3.5% of a (below 2% after nine)  (2.0s) runs=1, T2_over_a=0.4966, worst_excess_after=0.01721, levels=6
